@subsection An Example of Compiled Code

Now compile a recursive factorial program through the public source entry
point. @code{compileAndRun} admits the source, compiles it, and runs the
instruction stream:

@example typescript
const factorialRun = compileAndRun(`
  function factorial(n: number): number @{
    return n <= 1 ? 1 : factorial(n - 1) * n;
  @}
  console.log(factorial(5));
`);
factorialRun.transcript;
// => ["120"]
@end example

@noindent
For an admitted program, @code{compileProgram} returns a
@code{CompiledProgram}. It contains one instruction sequence per top-level form
and a flat @code{instructions} array. The program compiler uses
@code{compileStatement} for each form. The expression compiler dispatches on
checked syntax nodes and emits typed machine statements. The compiler does not
take a target or linkage option at the program entry point.

@example
⟨@emph{save @code{env} if modified by code to compute value}⟩
  ⟨@emph{compilation of definition value, 
   target @code{val}, linkage @code{next}}⟩
  ⟨@emph{restore @code{env} if saved above}⟩
assign(
  "val",
  op(
    "define-variable",
    constant("factorial"),
    register("val"),
    register("env")
  )
),
assign("val", register("val")),
  @r{; the defined value stays in @code{val}}
@end example

@noindent
A function declaration compiles through @code{compileStatement} and the
compiler's internal declaration generator. The generator uses
@code{compileLambda} to create a closure and emit its body behind an entry
label. The closure stores the current environment. At run time, the call
compiler extends that environment with the procedure parameters and arguments.

@example typescript
