@{ tag: "label", name: "entry2" @},
⟨@emph{compilation of procedure body}⟩
@{ tag: "label", name: "after-lambda1" @},
assign(
  "val",
  op(
    "make-procedure",
    constant("entry2"),
    constant(["n"]),
    register("env"),
  )
),
assign(
  "val",
  op(
    "define-variable",
    constant("factorial"),
    register("val"),
    register("env")
  )
),
assign("val", register("val")),
@end example

@noindent
The compiler uses @code{compileSequence} for a procedure body and gives the
final form @code{returnLinkage}. For a conditional expression,
@code{compileConditional} compiles its test and branches. The branches produce
the procedure result in @code{val}.

@example
⟨@emph{save @code{continue}, @code{env} if modified by 
 predicate and needed by branches}⟩
  ⟨@emph{compilation of predicate, 
   target @code{val}, linkage @code{next}}⟩
  ⟨@emph{restore @code{continue}, @code{env} if saved above}⟩
test("boolean-not", register("val")),
branch("false-branch4"),
@{ tag: "label", name: "true-branch5" @},
  ⟨@emph{compilation of true branch, 
   target @code{val}, linkage @code{return}}⟩
@{ tag: "label", name: "false-branch4" @},
  ⟨@emph{compilation of false branch, 
   target @code{val}, linkage @code{return}}⟩
@{ tag: "label", name: "after-if3" @},
@end example

@noindent
The predicate @code{n <= 1} is not a call in this edition: the guest's
binary operators compile directly to machine operations. The generated code
evaluates @code{n} into @code{val}, stages it through @code{item} while the
constant 1 is loaded, and applies @code{number-less-equal}. The test then
dispatches to the false branch while the true branch falls through. The
requirements to preserve registers around the evaluation don't result in any
saving of registers beyond the @code{item} staging, because in this case
those evaluations don't modify the registers in question.

@example typescript
